The static audit has found a material soundness failure, not merely a coverage limitation: the K `exactNum` addition/subtraction rules model `Decimal` arithmetic as unbounded exact rational arithmetic, while the real Python program uses the ambient decimal context (default precision 28). For a valid 28-digit input ending in `.4`, K returns the exact-nearest integer but the submitted Python function rounds during `± 0.5` first and returns a different integer. I’m preserving both positive and negative false-conclusion witnesses and completing the rest of the mandatory audit.
